## Phase 1 — Create the Permissions Lab

Created the project directory:

mkdir ~/permissions-lab
cd ~/permissions-lab

Created three initial test files:

touch public.txt
touch analyst.txt
touch developer.txt

Verified the files:

ls -l

## Phase 2 — Configure File Permissions

Modified the permissions of the test files:

chmod 644 public.txt
chmod 600 analyst.txt
chmod 640 developer.txt

These permissions were used to demonstrate different levels of access.

### Permission Breakdown

644:
- Owner: Read and Write
- Group: Read
- Others: Read

600:
- Owner: Read and Write
- Group: No Access
- Others: No Access

640:
- Owner: Read and Write
- Group: Read
- Others: No Access

## Phase 3 — Configure Ownership and Groups

Configured group ownership for the files:

sudo chown dedfr:cybersecurity analyst.txt
sudo chown dedfr:developers developer.txt
sudo chown dedfr:cybersecurity public.txt

Verified the groups:

getent group cybersecurity
getent group developers

Verified ownership and permissions:

ls -l

The files were successfully assigned to the appropriate Linux groups.

## Phase 4 — Verify File Metadata

Used the stat command to inspect detailed information about the files:

stat analyst.txt
stat developer.txt

This verified:
- File ownership
- Group ownership
- Permission mode
- File size
- Inode information
- Access timestamps
- Modification timestamps

## Phase 5 — Create a Shared File

Created a file intended for controlled group access:

echo "Shared cybersecurity lab file" > shared.txt

Configured ownership:

sudo chown dedfr:cybersecurity shared.txt

Configured permissions:

chmod 660 shared.txt

Verified the file:

cat shared.txt
ls -l shared.txt
stat shared.txt

The resulting permissions were:

-rw-rw----

This means:
- Owner: Read and Write
- Group: Read and Write
- Others: No Access

This demonstrated how group permissions can be used to provide controlled access to shared resources.

## Phase 6 — Configure Directory Permissions

Changed the permissions of the project directory:

chmod 750 /home/dedfr/permissions-lab

Verified the directory:

ls -ld /home/dedfr/permissions-lab

The 750 permission configuration provides:

- Owner: Read, Write, Execute
- Group: Read, Execute
- Others: No Access

Directory execute permission is important because users generally need execute permission on a directory to traverse it and access files inside it.

## Phase 7 — Final File Permissions

Configured the final permissions:

chmod 700 analyst.txt
chmod 640 developer.txt
chmod 644 public.txt
chmod 660 shared.txt

Final configuration:

analyst.txt
- Permissions: 700
- Owner: dedfr
- Group: cybersecurity

developer.txt
