- Owner: dedfr
- Group: developers

public.txt
- Permissions: 644
- Owner: dedfr
- Group: cybersecurity

shared.txt
- Permissions: 660
- Owner: dedfr
- Group: cybersecurity

Final directory permissions:

750

## Permission Testing and Troubleshooting

During access testing, the analyst account received Permission denied errors when attempting to access files inside the permissions-lab directory.

This demonstrated an important Linux security concept: file permissions are only one part of access control. A user must also have the appropriate permissions on the parent directory in order to reach a file.

The permission-denied results were documented as part of the troubleshooting process.

## Linux Permission Structure

Linux file permissions are divided into three categories:

Owner | Group | Others

The three primary permissions are:

r = Read
w = Write
x = Execute

For example:

-rw-rw----

means:

Owner = Read + Write
Group = Read + Write
Others = No Access

